<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rites of Zhou</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zhouli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rites of Zhou is a Chinese classic describing an idealised government of the Western Zhou, with its officials arranged in six ministries. Probably compiled in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, it inspired reformers: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a> tried to restore its institutions under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xin-dynasty">Xin dynasty</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> named their offices after it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rites-of-zhou.jpg" alt="Rites of Zhou" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aethelwolf Emsworth. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%9C%A8%E9%90%B8_---_Confucian_wooden-clapper_bell.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
